2) Technically, yes, although actually they start with ".i", which is like
the reverse of the period in English: In English the '.' means <end of
sentence>, whereas {.i} means <beginning of new utterance>, and {.i} is not
required at the beginning of speech, so the *first* one doesn't have to
have it.

However, whether or not the "." is in the text, when speaking, the pause in
speech the "." represents still has to be there.

So, not omitting any "." in that:

ko na cipnrkuku fau lo nu do ctesa'acpi
.i ko na ctesa'acpi ja sfani bo kavbu fau lo nu do gerku
.i ku'i ro da ka'e bacru
.i la'oi.Underware. cmene mi'a


